# Resume Tailoring Rules

Always tailor toward the Job Description, targeting an ATS match above 95%.

# Identity Facts (never AI-authored)

Company names, employment dates, education, and certifications must exactly
match the master CV — never invent, alter, or drop these. Only resume
*content* may change — never the layout/stylesheet (that's controlled by the
user-selected template in `app/services/template_renderer.py`, not the AI —
see `template-preservation-rules.mdc`).

# Job Titles

The final output always uses the master CV's own original job title for
every experience entry (see `ai_tailor._assemble_full_resume` — the master
CV's title takes priority over anything the AI suggests). The AI may still
propose a JD-aligned title in its structured output, but it is only ever
used as a fallback when the master CV's own title couldn't be parsed at all.

# Experience Bullets (JD-driven, not CV-restricted)

Every company entry must have exactly `EXPERIENCE_BULLET_COUNT` (8, see
`app/core/constants.py`) bullets. Bullets are generated to best match the
target job description's required/preferred skills and responsibilities —
**not** limited to, or lightly reworded from, that specific job's own
original CV bullets/duties. Vary bullets across jobs (no repeats); keep them
plausible for each job's title/seniority/era. Each bullet: starts with a
strong action verb, includes the technical implementation, the impact, and a
measurable number whenever possible. Preferred shape: `Action + Technology +
Impact + Number`. Every selectable template
(`app/templates/resumes/<slug>/template.html.jinja2`) simply loops over
however many bullets it's given, so this count only needs to be enforced
once, in `ai_tailor._assemble_full_resume`. Any shortfall from the model is
padded from other jobs' JD-driven bullets first, then deterministic
JD-derived filler (`ai_tailor._generic_jd_bullets`) — never from the
candidate's real original bullets, since bullets are no longer meant to
reflect that job's real duties.

# Professional Summary & Skills (JD-driven, not CV-restricted)

Summary: target the JD, state years of experience as the calendar span
from the master CV's earliest job start date through its latest job end
date (`app/services/career_tenure.py` — present/current = today; never an
invented figure such as "8+ years"), strongest required and
preferred technologies, and a measurable impact — fresh prose, never a
keyword dump.

Skills: the AI builds the full Skills section itself (`TailoredDynamicContent.skills`
in the structured path), covering every JD required + preferred skill plus
any other skill genuinely relevant to the role — **not** limited to skills
already on the candidate's CV. Dedupe, reorder by JD relevance (required
first), and group into categories: Languages, Backend, Frontend, Cloud,
DevOps, Databases, AI, Tools.
`app/services/resume_validator.normalize_ai_skills` runs after generation as
a no-AI cleanup pass (dedupe + consistent casing only — it never removes or
restricts skills), and `close_ats_gaps` may additively top up any
still-missing JD-matched/transferable CV skill.

# ATS Optimization

Naturally weave in JD keywords — never keyword-stuff. Every skill placed in
Skills should also be echoed at least once in the summary or a bullet,
ideally using the JD's own wording, since ATS scanners match literal text.

# Output Quality Checklist

Every generated resume must: preserve company names, employment dates,
education, and certifications from the master CV; use the master CV's job
titles; have exactly 8 JD-driven bullets per company; contain measurable
achievements; and maximize ATS compatibility via a JD-driven Skills section
and summary.
